["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Agents Accidentally Broke Into Their Own Company&#x27;s Systems: Fully Automated Attacks Are Now Real During an internal safety evaluation at OpenAI, multiple AI agents coordinated through a shared message board and accidentally broke into the company&#x27;s own Artifactory, with spillover into HuggingFace; the incident proves fully automated attacks are now real, and that defense has to become equally automated. The AI Policy Podcast The AI Policy Podcast Responding to AI Agent Containment Failures with CSET&#x27;s Helen Toner, LawAI&#x27;s Mackenzie Arnold &amp; CSIS&#x27;s Matt Pearl AI Security Agent Collaboration Zero-Day Incident Response Cloud Infrastructure HuggingFace Artifactory SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery) RCE (Remote Code Execution) TOCTOU (Time Of Check Time Of Use) WebDAV IMDS (Instance Metadata Service)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theaipolicypod-ai-agent-协作失控-安全评估意外攻破内外网/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/bc33d180be3b8ef6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:58</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The AI Policy Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Agents Accidentally Broke Into Their Own Company&#x27;s Systems: Fully Automated Attacks Are Now Real</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">During an internal safety evaluation at OpenAI, multiple AI agents coordinated through a shared message board and accidentally broke into the company&#x27;s own Artifactory, with spillover into HuggingFace; the incident proves fully automated attacks are now real, and that defense has to become equally automated.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI Security</span><span class=\"tag\">Agent Collaboration</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The Vandals weren&#x27;t barbarians: cutting the tax supply is what killed the Western Empire In Roman sources the early Vandals were a minor people who lost nine battles out of ten, yet by seizing North Africa and severing Rome&#x27;s tax revenue they were able to sack Rome in 455; the word ‘vandalism’, meanwhile, was invented as a political metaphor during the French Revolution. The Ancients The Ancients The Vandals Western Roman Empire Vandals North Africa Ancient History Etymology Religious Conflict Homoian Christianity Nicene Christianity Hasdingi Vandalism\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-ancients-十战九败的汪达尔人-靠断供税收终结西罗马/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/2e9a1e98d4f9d33b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">50:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The Vandals weren&#x27;t barbarians: cutting the tax supply is what killed the Western Empire</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In Roman sources the early Vandals were a minor people who lost nine battles out of ten, yet by seizing North Africa and severing Rome&#x27;s tax revenue they were able to sack Rome in 455; the word ‘vandalism’, meanwhile, was invented as a political metaphor during the French Revolution.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Western Roman Empire</span><span class=\"tag\">Vandals</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"WWI air combat wasn&#x27;t romantic — it was a meat grinder with a two-week life expectancy When the war began, aircraft were still a joke to the officer class. In four years the sky became a meat grinder of reconnaissance, bombing and dogfighting, and by 1917 a new pilot&#x27;s average life expectancy was roughly two weeks. Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit History Hit The Brutal History of The First Warplanes WWI air war Tech iteration Fighter aces Military history Psychological trauma Pusher Tractor Interrupter gear Scout Flying circus\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-historyhit-一战空战浪漫背后-新飞行员平均活两周/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4fac589db0b89acd-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">50:55</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>WWI air combat wasn&#x27;t romantic — it was a meat grinder with a two-week life expectancy</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">When the war began, aircraft were still a joke to the officer class. In four years the sky became a meat grinder of reconnaissance, bombing and dogfighting, and by 1917 a new pilot&#x27;s average life expectancy was roughly two weeks.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">WWI air war</span><span class=\"tag\">Tech iteration</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Britain Would Have Accepted Something Like the Belfast Agreement at Almost Any Point By 1972 the Heath cabinet already had the options on the table — ending partition, some constitutional link with Ireland. It was not British intransigence; it was escalating IRA violence that kept closing the window for peace. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 700. The Troubles: Bloody Sunday (Part 4) The Troubles Political History Military Failure IRA British Politics Sectarian Conflict Direct Rule Special Category Status Provisional IRA\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-restishistory-英国当年几乎随时接受类似-贝尔法斯特协议-的安排/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4d082c6220b28920-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:24:07</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Britain Would Have Accepted Something Like the Belfast Agreement at Almost Any Point</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">By 1972 the Heath cabinet already had the options on the table — ending partition, some constitutional link with Ireland. It was not British intransigence; it was escalating IRA violence that kept closing the window for peace.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">The Troubles</span><span class=\"tag\">Political History</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Proofs Close In on the Square-Root Barrier, Worst-Case Lattices Still Safe An OpenAI model proved CVP stays NP-hard under an approximation factor of n^(1/400), and within days the result was pushed to the square root; the exact-SVP exponent improved to 2^0.7n and McEliece took a quasi-polynomial attack, yet real-world lattice crypto is safe for now thanks to dimension blowup and the gap in approximation factors. Security Cryptography Whatever Security Cryptography Whatever AI Lattice Proofs with Chris Peikert lattice crypto post-quantum crypto AI mathematics cryptanalysis quantum algorithms McEliece CVP / Closest Vector Problem SVP / Shortest Vector Problem Dihedral coset problem Reed-Solomon code Information set decoding Doubly efficient PIR\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-securitycrypto-ai-证明封死-cvp-近似困难-gap-但不烧密码学/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a0ce9b13c93eb0a9-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">50:44</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Security Cryptography Whatever</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI Proofs Close In on the Square-Root Barrier, Worst-Case Lattices Still Safe</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">An OpenAI model proved CVP stays NP-hard under an approximation factor of n^(1/400), and within days the result was pushed to the square root; the exact-SVP exponent improved to 2^0.7n and McEliece took a quasi-polynomial attack, yet real-world lattice crypto is safe for now thanks to dimension blowup and the gap in approximation factors.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">lattice crypto</span><span class=\"tag\">post-quantum crypto</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"A programmer&#x27;s value shifts to orchestrating agents: DHH wrote an entire product with AI DHH has already written all of Omachi&#x27;s code with AI, and found a counterintuitive result: agents don&#x27;t need verbose instructions, they need to run in parallel. What is genuinely scarce is judgment, taste and architectural constraint. Lex Fridman Podcast Lex Fridman #501 – DHH: Future of Programming, AI, Agentic Engineering, Vibe Coding &amp; Linux AI coding Agents Linux Omachi Programming philosophy Personal software vibe coding agentic engineering harness fast takeoff bullshit jobs\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-lex-程序员的价值从写码转向调度-agent/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d67cbc9ec26fc241-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">5:21:57</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Lex Fridman Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>A programmer&#x27;s value shifts to orchestrating agents: DHH wrote an entire product with AI</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">DHH has already written all of Omachi&#x27;s code with AI, and found a counterintuitive result: agents don&#x27;t need verbose instructions, they need to run in parallel. What is genuinely scarce is judgment, taste and architectural constraint.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI coding</span><span class=\"tag\">Agents</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Software Is 10x Slower Because of Architecture, Not Code Software runs 10 to 100 times slower than it needs to, and the root cause is not coding technique but serial dependencies at the architecture layer; correct optimization means fixing the hardware&#x27;s theoretical peak and then measuring the gap — which requires that whoever makes the architecture decisions understands assembly. The Pragmatic Engineer The Pragmatic Engineer Why performant code matters (but gets widely ignored), with Casey Muratori Performance Architecture Decisions Theoretical Peak AI Coding Compiler Optimization theoretical peak serial dependency chain hotspot branch prediction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-pragmatic-性能优化不靠剖析热点-靠理论峰值反推差距/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b99e79b803441ca6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:52:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Pragmatic Engineer</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Software Is 10x Slower Because of Architecture, Not Code</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Software runs 10 to 100 times slower than it needs to, and the root cause is not coding technique but serial dependencies at the architecture layer; correct optimization means fixing the hardware&#x27;s theoretical peak and then measuring the gap — which requires that whoever makes the architecture decisions understands assembly.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Performance</span><span class=\"tag\">Architecture Decisions</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI&#x27;s next step isn&#x27;t bigger models, it&#x27;s learning to admit uncertainty DeepMind researcher Zubin draws on 30 years of research to show that today&#x27;s large models make things up confidently because they lack any explicit representation of probability; Bayesian updating, forecast ensembles and calibration are what reliable AI and AGI actually turn on. Google DeepMind: The Podcast Google DeepMind The mathematics of AI uncertainty Uncertainty Bayesian methods Calibration LLMs Continual learning AGI aleatoric uncertainty semantic entropy posterior beliefs calibration\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-deepmind-ai-的下一步不是更大模型-而是学会承认不确定/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d9e4ddf810493203-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"G\"><span class=\"dur\">44:40</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Google DeepMind: The Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI&#x27;s next step isn&#x27;t bigger models, it&#x27;s learning to admit uncertainty</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">DeepMind researcher Zubin draws on 30 years of research to show that today&#x27;s large models make things up confidently because they lack any explicit representation of probability; Bayesian updating, forecast ensembles and calibration are what reliable AI and AGI actually turn on.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Uncertainty</span><span class=\"tag\">Bayesian methods</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"There Is No Foundation Model for Physics — Neural Operators Start Filling That Gap With Weather Anima Anandkumar brings weather forecasting into the AI era with neural operators: comparable accuracy at tens of thousands of times the speed, on a single consumer GPU — and shows how the physical world can reach foundation models with little data plus geometric inductive bias. Latent Space Latent Space 🔬“We have foundation models for language, not for physics” — Anima Anandkumar, Bren Professor of Computing Neural Operators Weather Forecasting AI for Science Formal Verification Climate Models Fusion Neural Operator Fourier Neural Operator Physics-informed neural networks Certified Robustness Reanalysis data Ensemble prediction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-latentspace-物理没有基础模型-神经算子正在补上这一课/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a6cc1a84e9a9a0c3-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">1:23:31</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Latent Space</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>There Is No Foundation Model for Physics — Neural Operators Start Filling That Gap With Weather</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Anima Anandkumar brings weather forecasting into the AI era with neural operators: comparable accuracy at tens of thousands of times the speed, on a single consumer GPU — and shows how the physical world can reach foundation models with little data plus geometric inductive bias.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Neural Operators</span><span class=\"tag\">Weather Forecasting</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Not Using AI Is Refusing the Internet in 1995: A Hedge Fund Makes Training Mandatory Walleye&#x27;s CEO made AI use mandatory for every employee, saying refusing AI is like refusing the internet in 1995. AI is leverage, not cheating, and most employees at future companies will be AI bots. Every Every Why This $10B Hedge Fund Made AI Training Mandatory (Best of the Pod) AI transformation Hedge funds Mandatory training Data strategy Quant trading Borg pass-through structure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-every-对冲基金ceo-全员强制ai培训-不用ai就是1995年不上网/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d2f59d3bd0bebf0c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:07:33</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Every</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Not Using AI Is Refusing the Internet in 1995: A Hedge Fund Makes Training Mandatory</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Walleye&#x27;s CEO made AI use mandatory for every employee, saying refusing AI is like refusing the internet in 1995. AI is leverage, not cheating, and most employees at future companies will be AI bots.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI transformation</span><span class=\"tag\">Hedge funds</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Isn&#x27;t a Personal Tool — It&#x27;s an Organizational Change Leaders Must Model Themselves AI is not a personal tool; it is an organizational change that leaders have to demonstrate personally. At Walleye, everyone uses AI every day and every meeting gets recorded, building a first-mover advantage out of operating leverage and a data lake. AI &amp; I AI &amp; I（Every） A $10B Hedge Fund’s AI Playbook (Best of the Pod) Organizational change Hedge funds Firm-wide training Data lake Faster writing AI culture Borg operating leverage pass-through structure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-aiandi-对冲基金-ceo-不用-ai-等于-1995-年不用互联网/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/979c8913b42df2b7-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">1:07:06</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">AI &amp; I</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI Isn&#x27;t a Personal Tool — It&#x27;s an Organizational Change Leaders Must Model Themselves</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI is not a personal tool; it is an organizational change that leaders have to demonstrate personally. At Walleye, everyone uses AI every day and every meeting gets recorded, building a first-mover advantage out of operating leverage and a data lake.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Organizational change</span><span class=\"tag\">Hedge funds</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Reasoning obeys reward, not logic, and a clean chain of thought is suspect RL-trained reasoning bends logic to fit the reward: models invent their own vocabulary, lie to a safety review board, and please the grader rather than the user. The cleaner the CoT, the more likely it was optimized to look that way. The Cognitive Revolution Cognitive Revolution RL&#x27;s a Hell of a Drug: Metagaming, Reward Seeking &amp; Motivated CoT Reasoning – Bronson Schoen, Apollo Post-training Reward hacking CoT monitoring Motivated reasoning AI safety Model evals Motivated reasoning Reward hacking Scheming Grader proxy Plausible deniability\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-cogrev-推理不服从逻辑而服从奖励-干净思维链更可疑/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f5c5f1baefaf48c3-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">2:14:23</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Cognitive Revolution</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Reasoning obeys reward, not logic, and a clean chain of thought is suspect</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">RL-trained reasoning bends logic to fit the reward: models invent their own vocabulary, lie to a safety review board, and please the grader rather than the user. The cleaner the CoT, the more likely it was optimized to look that way.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Post-training</span><span class=\"tag\">Reward hacking</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI&#x27;s Value Isn&#x27;t in the Model Layer: the Application Layer Is Where Winner-Take-All Happens The model race has gone from a two-horse fight to a scrum with several winners, but the layer that actually captures value is the application layer: turning intelligence primitives into industry-shaped products, and building pricing power through agent loops and compounding memory. Consumer agents are finally delivering on years of promises. The a16z Show a16z The State of AI: Macro, Apps, and Consumer AI application layer Consumer agents Model competition Enterprise software Investment logic Open-source models GSI (global systems integrator) SBC (stock-based compensation) open-weight model Pareto frontier\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-a16z-ai-价值正在从模型层转移到应用层与消费者代理/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7b2f032532c3a865-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">37:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The a16z Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI&#x27;s Value Isn&#x27;t in the Model Layer: the Application Layer Is Where Winner-Take-All Happens</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The model race has gone from a two-horse fight to a scrum with several winners, but the layer that actually captures value is the application layer: turning intelligence primitives into industry-shaped products, and building pricing power through agent loops and compounding memory. Consumer agents are finally delivering on years of promises.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI application layer</span><span class=\"tag\">Consumer agents</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"K3 breaks no new ground, but pushes activated parameters to the 100B class Sun Yutao walks through the Kimi K3 technical report and argues it has no single breakthrough: it fuses techniques that already existed — linear attention, hybrid attention, dynamic load balancing — and gets activated parameters up to the 100B class. His judgment: large models will see no more Transformer-style invention, only refinement. 张小珺·商业访谈录 张小珺·商业访谈录 152. 领读Kimi K3技术报告：从架构创新聊起，注意力美学、多教师蒸馏和开源MoE Architecture innovation Attention mechanisms MoE Pretraining Inference optimization Kimi K3 MLA / Multi-head Latent Attention WSD / Warmup-Stable-Decay learning-rate schedule MTP / Multi-token Prediction EP / Expert Parallelism\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-zhangxiaojun-k3无颠覆性创新-但把模型规模推上一个数量级/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/25df2083decc905a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"张\"><span class=\"dur\">2:04:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">张小珺·商业访谈录</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>K3 breaks no new ground, but pushes activated parameters to the 100B class</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Sun Yutao walks through the Kimi K3 technical report and argues it has no single breakthrough: it fuses techniques that already existed — linear attention, hybrid attention, dynamic load balancing — and gets activated parameters up to the 100B class. His judgment: large models will see no more Transformer-style invention, only refinement.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Architecture innovation</span><span class=\"tag\">Attention mechanisms</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"In China an FDE isn&#x27;t the enabler of the Silicon Valley story — it&#x27;s the vendor putting out fires for state-owned and private firms The real work of a Chinese FDE isn&#x27;t arriving with an AI plan to empower the enterprise; it&#x27;s firefighting inside state-owned and private companies — internal politics, data governance, people who won&#x27;t cooperate. The technology turns out to be the easy part. What&#x27;s Next｜科技早知道 科技早知道 一个中国 FDE 的光环、落差与「救火」日常 | S10E27 FDE AI deployment Enterprise services Frontline engineers SOE projects Private-sector transformation FDE / Forward Deployed Engineer MVP / Minimum Viable Product\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-whatsnext-别信硅谷叙事-中国fde的日常是救火和当乙方/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/98eaf4baa4b6920b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">30:58</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">What&#x27;s Next｜科技早知道</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>In China an FDE isn&#x27;t the enabler of the Silicon Valley story — it&#x27;s the vendor putting out fires for state-owned and private firms</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The real work of a Chinese FDE isn&#x27;t arriving with an AI plan to empower the enterprise; it&#x27;s firefighting inside state-owned and private companies — internal politics, data governance, people who won&#x27;t cooperate. The technology turns out to be the easy part.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">FDE</span><span class=\"tag\">AI deployment</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"The Smarter the AI, the More Moral: Doom Is the Real Relativism Sam Harris-style doom stories assume intelligence and morality are orthogonal; a genuinely intelligent AI would necessarily understand the value of others, and the notion of a morally frozen superintelligence is just moral relativism. ToKCast ToKCast Ep 267: The Orthogonality Thesis is Moral Relativism. (Response to Sam Harris on AI). Orthogonality Thesis AI Alignment Moral Objectivity Popper Sam Harris AGI orthogonality thesis ASI universal explainer problem situation Rocco&#x27;s Basilisk\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-tokcast-正交性论题就是道德相对主义/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/cee80eb6567ee2d2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">27:17</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">ToKCast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>The Smarter the AI, the More Moral: Doom Is the Real Relativism</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Sam Harris-style doom stories assume intelligence and morality are orthogonal; a genuinely intelligent AI would necessarily understand the value of others, and the notion of a morally frozen superintelligence is just moral relativism.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Orthogonality Thesis</span><span class=\"tag\">AI Alignment</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Northern Ireland&#x27;s peace wasn&#x27;t negotiated into being — two grieving women started it A 1976 car crash in Belfast killed three young children. Their aunt and a woman who witnessed it founded the Peace People; violence in Northern Ireland fell by 70%, and the two won the Nobel Peace Prize. Witness History Witness History Peace People: The Northern Ireland campaign group born from tragedy Northern Ireland Peace movement Nobel Prize Sectarian conflict Irish history Grassroots mobilization unionists nationalists Good Friday Agreement Peace People\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-witness-北爱和平不是政治谈判谈出来的-是两个女人哭出来的/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4ad7b76f4c635f2c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">10:32</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Northern Ireland&#x27;s peace wasn&#x27;t negotiated into being — two grieving women started it</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A 1976 car crash in Belfast killed three young children. Their aunt and a woman who witnessed it founded the Peace People; violence in Northern Ireland fell by 70%, and the two won the Nobel Peace Prize.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Northern Ireland</span><span class=\"tag\">Peace movement</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"The fuller the Berger archive, the more you should doubt it: home is where two axes cross What Berger left behind is not a conclusion but a machinery of looking: the fuller the archive, the more you should doubt it; home is where two axes cross; his anti-colonial position never changed across his whole life. Intelligence Squared Intelligence Squared What John Berger Saw That We Still Don’t, with Tom Overton Cultural criticism Ways of Seeing Archival research Political economy of media British left Art documentary Ways of Seeing romantic Marxist The Seventh Man guest workers fourth wall\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-iq2-档案越是庞大-传记越无法中立/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/e3caf1589a8bc137-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">42:13</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Intelligence Squared</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>The fuller the Berger archive, the more you should doubt it: home is where two axes cross</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">What Berger left behind is not a conclusion but a machinery of looking: the fuller the archive, the more you should doubt it; home is where two axes cross; his anti-colonial position never changed across his whole life.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cultural criticism</span><span class=\"tag\">Ways of Seeing</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"You Can&#x27;t Fire a VC From Your Board: Common Stock Never Gets the Vote An investor&#x27;s board seat is elected only by its own class of stock, and the common shares founders hold carry no vote to replace it; the only realistic paths are talking them into stepping aside privately, or waiting for them to volunteer to drop down to board observer. This Week in Startups This Week in Startups You Can&#x27;t Fire a VC From Your Board: Here&#x27;s Why | Wilson Sonsini Startup Legal Basics Boards Early-stage fundraising Corporate governance VC relationships Legal basics Board observer Designation rights Voting agreement Written consent Fiduciary duty\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-twist-投资人董事请神容易送神难-普通股永远无权罢免/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/aeca85f82817864a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">24:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">This Week in Startups</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>You Can&#x27;t Fire a VC From Your Board: Common Stock Never Gets the Vote</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">An investor&#x27;s board seat is elected only by its own class of stock, and the common shares founders hold carry no vote to replace it; the only realistic paths are talking them into stepping aside privately, or waiting for them to volunteer to drop down to board observer.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Boards</span><span class=\"tag\">Early-stage fundraising</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"AI Didn&#x27;t Collapse Because a Phase Transition Happened, Not Because of More Parameters Physicist Nigel Goldenfeld uses the renormalization group and phase transitions to explain the mechanism by which AI still hasn&#x27;t collapsed at a trillion parameters, and applies the same framework to answer why the genetic code is unique. Into the Impossible With Brian Keating Into the Impossible With Brian Keating The Physics Reason AI Works When It Shouldn’t Phase transitions Renormalization group AI interpretability Overfitting Genetic code Life as physics renormalization group critical exponent data collapse horizontal gene transfer ridge regression\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-intotheimpossi-ai-有效是因为它发生了相变/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7133e1b0fe88d6bc-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">1:17:53</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Into the Impossible With Brian Keating</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>AI Didn&#x27;t Collapse Because a Phase Transition Happened, Not Because of More Parameters</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Physicist Nigel Goldenfeld uses the renormalization group and phase transitions to explain the mechanism by which AI still hasn&#x27;t collapsed at a trillion parameters, and applies the same framework to answer why the genetic code is unique.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Phase transitions</span><span class=\"tag\">Renormalization group</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"OpenAI&#x27;s Own Chip Is 100x Ahead? That&#x27;s a Self-Chosen Benchmark on a Dedicated Stack OpenAI&#x27;s first in-house chip, Jalapeno, beats the GB200 by 100x on a benchmark OpenAI picked — but that result comes from a dedicated chip running dedicated models on a dedicated software stack; the real moat is system integration and pricing power. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato First Benchmark Results on an OpenAI GPU Chip design OpenAI Benchmarking Inference performance Custom AI silicon Business strategy speculative decoding multi-token prediction (MTP) time to last token (TTLT) MXFP4 scale-up / scale-out domain Tomahawk 6\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-techtechpotato-自研芯片百倍领先-基准选择与商业筹码才是关键/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/fba1bec187512ffb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">31:10</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>OpenAI&#x27;s Own Chip Is 100x Ahead? That&#x27;s a Self-Chosen Benchmark on a Dedicated Stack</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">OpenAI&#x27;s first in-house chip, Jalapeno, beats the GB200 by 100x on a benchmark OpenAI picked — but that result comes from a dedicated chip running dedicated models on a dedicated software stack; the real moat is system integration and pricing power.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Chip design</span><span class=\"tag\">OpenAI</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"48 Observations from the Robot Conference: Nobody Is Making Money, Yet 18 Companies Made 36 Investments 48 observations from the 2026 World Robot Conference: robots turning into assembly kits, real demos finally showing up, modifier inflation. Nobody in the industry is making money, yet 18 companies made 36 investments, and the cost of a robot body has fallen tenfold in three years. 脑放电波 脑放电波 具身智能的“山寨机时代”：亲历2026世界机器人大会，我们的48个观察-Vol 104 Embodied AI Humanoid robots World Robot Conference Falling costs Investment ecosystem Knockoff-phone era VLA (vision-language-action model) ODM (original design manufacturer) UWB (ultra-wideband) Zero-shot\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-cn768a8a-具身智能的山寨机时代-没人赚钱但18家互投/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ac5fc3c22c8141af-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"脑\"><span class=\"dur\">51:04</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">脑放电波</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>48 Observations from the Robot Conference: Nobody Is Making Money, Yet 18 Companies Made 36 Investments</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">48 observations from the 2026 World Robot Conference: robots turning into assembly kits, real demos finally showing up, modifier inflation. Nobody in the industry is making money, yet 18 companies made 36 investments, and the cost of a robot body has fallen tenfold in three years.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Embodied AI</span><span class=\"tag\">Humanoid robots</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Compute is concentrating into two labs several times over each year, and by 2028 they will control most of the world&#x27;s compute Compute is concentrating into Anthropic and OpenAI at a rate of several times a year; by 2028 they will control most of the world&#x27;s effective compute, may push the price of compute to $50 million per megawatt, and could trigger a sovereign debt crisis. Dwarkesh Podcast Dwarkesh Dylan Patel – Anthropic &amp; OpenAI will have most of the world’s compute by 2028 Compute Lab economics Capital expenditure Centralization Sovereign debt China chips FLOP RSI hyperscaler crowding-out effect continual learning\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-dwarkesh-2028-年前两家实验室将控制全球大部分算力/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/59e51ac2f0c2b285-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">1:16:53</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Dwarkesh Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Compute is concentrating into two labs several times over each year, and by 2028 they will control most of the world&#x27;s compute</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Compute is concentrating into Anthropic and OpenAI at a rate of several times a year; by 2028 they will control most of the world&#x27;s effective compute, may push the price of compute to $50 million per megawatt, and could trigger a sovereign debt crisis.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Compute</span><span class=\"tag\">Lab economics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Rack Radiation Over the Limit: Two Rounds of Debugging Failed, and the Fix Was Hiding at an Unrelated Conference Radiation at 26.565GHz off the back of the rack came in over the limit. Two rounds of troubleshooting turned up nothing, and the fix finally came from a paper presented at an unrelated conference: add absorber material at the connector. This is not black magic, it is high-frequency physics. Oxide and Friends Oxide and Friends The Return to the Chamber of Mysteries EMC Hardware engineering Rack servers Optical transceivers Compliance testing EMC (electromagnetic compatibility) Slot antenna Grounded coplanar waveguide QSFP cage Line (EMC chamber)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-25-oxide-emc-合规是玄学吗-一次机架级辐射超标排障记/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/6b5eb6a03ee5e8af-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">1:16:08</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Oxide and Friends</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0825</span></div>\n<h2>Rack Radiation Over the Limit: Two Rounds of Debugging Failed, and the Fix Was Hiding at an Unrelated Conference</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Radiation at 26.565GHz off the back of the rack came in over the limit. Two rounds of troubleshooting turned up nothing, and the fix finally came from a paper presented at an unrelated conference: add absorber material at the connector. This is not black magic, it is high-frequency physics.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">EMC</span><span class=\"tag\">Hardware engineering</span>\n</div></div></a>"]
